## What are Interfaces?

An interface defines a set of methods that types can implement:

```xiom
pub interface Eq[T] {
  fn equals(self: &T, other: &T) -> Bool;
}
```

Any type implementing Eq can be compared with ==.

### Core Interfaces in XIOM

| Interface | Purpose | Key Method |
|-----------|---------|------------|
| Eq | Equality | equals |
| Ord | Ordering | compare |
| Display | String output | to_str |
| Clone | Deep copy | clone |
| Hash | Hashing | hash |
| Default | Default value | default |
| Iterator | Sequential access | next |
| From/Into | Type conversion | from / into |

### Implementing an Interface
```xiom
pub type Point = {
  x: Int;
  y: Int;
}

impl Eq[Point] for Point {
  fn equals(self: &Point, other: &Point) -> Bool {
    return self.x == other.x && self.y == other.y;
  }
}
```

### Using Interface Methods
```xiom
fn are_same_position[T: Eq](a: &T, b: &T) -> Bool {
  return a == b;  // Works for any T implementing Eq
}
```

### Interfaces vs Inheritance
Interfaces are NOT inheritance. They don't share data — only behavior contracts. A type can implement many interfaces independently.

